# Service (Business Logic)

Services are `@Service` annotated classes that contain **all business logic**. They orchestrate operations between repositories and model objects.

## Structure

```java
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final OrderRepository orderRepository;
    private final ProductRepository productRepository;

    public OrderService(
            OrderRepository orderRepository,
            ProductRepository productRepository
    ) {
        this.orderRepository = orderRepository;
        this.productRepository = productRepository;
    }

    @Transactional
    public Order createOrder(String customerName, Long productId, int quantity) {
        Product product = productRepository.findById(productId)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new ResourceNotFoundException("Product", productId));

        Order order = new Order(customerName);
        OrderItem item = new OrderItem(product, quantity);
        order.addItem(item);

        return orderRepository.save(order);
    }

    public Order getOrder(Long id) {
        return orderRepository.findById(id)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new ResourceNotFoundException("Order", id));
    }

    public List<Order> listOrders() {
        return orderRepository.findAll();
    }

    @Transactional
    public Order confirmOrder(Long id) {
        Order order = getOrder(id);
        order.confirm();
        return orderRepository.save(order);
    }

    @Transactional
    public void deleteOrder(Long id) {
        if (!orderRepository.existsById(id)) {
            throw new ResourceNotFoundException("Order", id);
        }
        orderRepository.deleteById(id);
    }
}
```

## Constructor Injection

Always use constructor injection. Spring auto-wires by type.

```java
// ❌ BAD — Field injection
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Autowired
    private OrderRepository orderRepository;
}

// ✅ GOOD — Constructor injection
@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final OrderRepository orderRepository;

    public OrderService(OrderRepository orderRepository) {
        this.orderRepository = orderRepository;
    }
}
```

## @Transactional

Use `@Transactional` on methods that **modify data**. Read-only methods don't need it.

```java
@Transactional
public Order createOrder(String customerName) {
    // Multiple DB operations within one transaction
}

public Order getOrder(Long id) {
    // Read-only — no @Transactional needed
}
```

## Method Naming

Service methods should express **business intent**:

```java
// ⚠️ WORSE — Generic CRUD names
public Order save(Order order) { }
public void delete(Long id) { }

// ✅ BETTER — Business intent
public Order createOrder(String customerName) { }
public Order confirmOrder(Long id) { }
public void cancelOrder(Long id) { }
```

## Error Handling

Services throw exceptions for business rule violations. The `GlobalExceptionHandler` maps them to HTTP responses.

```java
// Not found
throw new ResourceNotFoundException("Order", id);

// Business rule violation
throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot confirm an empty order");

// Validation
throw new IllegalArgumentException("Quantity must be positive");
```

See @.cursor/rules/design-errors.mdc for error handling patterns.

## Non-Negotiable Rules

### ❌ I will NEVER:
1. Call another Service from a Controller without going through the proper Service
2. Put HTTP concerns in Services (no `ResponseEntity`, no status codes)
3. Use field injection (`@Autowired` on fields)
4. Return `null` when an entity is not found (throw exception)

### ✅ I will ALWAYS:
1. Use `@Service` annotation
2. Use constructor injection for all dependencies
3. Use `@Transactional` for write operations
4. Throw meaningful exceptions for error cases
5. Name methods with business intent
